     2	
     3	Operational history of the Coverloop Multi-Model Production Protocol. The live doc (`CLAUDE.md`) carries only the current version; the story lives here.
     4	
     5	## 2026-09-04 — one command at the boundary (PROTOCOL v2.11.0)
     6	
     7	An evidence-driven review of the fleet's own logs asked whether Coverloop had
     8	become too heavy to follow. It had, in four specific ways — each fixed here,
     9	and none by weakening a gate.
    10	
    11	- **The tier is derived, not declared.** `attest` no longer takes the tier from
    12	  the caller: it classifies the same paths `gate` would, with the same floor
    13	  semantics, and records `tier_source`, the computed `floor` and its reasons.
    14	  Below the floor is refused (exit 2). `--raise-tier <T> --reason "<why>"`
    15	  elevates **upward only** and writes the reason into the report, for risk the
    16	  path classifier cannot see (a milestone gate covering many migrations, a
    17	  config file that steers auth). The legacy `--tier` pin still works above the
    18	  floor — recorded as an elevation with an explicit "no reason given" marker,
    19	  plus a migration note on stderr — so every pre-2.11 caller, including the four
    20	  SHA-pinned CI workflows, keeps passing unchanged.
    21	
    22	- **The baseline is unchanged, and deliberately conservative.** The walk still
    23	  demands a human signature before it will trust an ancestor's evidence. In the
    24	  fleet that drops 238 fully reviewed L3 commits from the baseline: their files
    25	  fall back into every later change's floor, so docs-only commits inherit L3
    26	  from the repo root and the floors are systematically inflated. Accepting
    27	  merely-VERIFIED evidence there was item 2 of this release, and it is
    28	  DEFERRED — see the review history below. Over-classification costs review
    29	  time; the alternative, as five rounds demonstrated, was a gate with a way
    30	  around it.
    31	
    32	- **`coverloop check` — one command at the merge boundary.** Refuse a dirty tree
    33	  → derive the tier → run the reviews that tier requires and capture their
    34	  transcripts → run the tests → bind to HEAD → run the real `gate`. It prints
    35	  `SAFE TO MERGE` or exactly **one** `STOP: <reason>`, and it is a wrapper over
    36	  the existing primitives with no new authority: `classify`, `attest` and `gate`
    37	  are untouched and still what CI runs. The reviewer lineage is configuration,
    38	  not architecture — `"reviewers": {"primary": "codex", "secondary": "glm"}`
    39	  with `"reviewer_commands"` — so Codex is a default, not an assumption. A
    40	  missing reviewer command is a STOP that names the role; a transcript that does
    41	  not end in an unambiguous pass marker is a `fail`, persisted as one.
    42	
    43	- **The session hook is now seven lines of invariants.** The SessionStart hook
    44	  ran on every session and after every compaction, and at 6 KB it turned the
    45	  builder into a process manager: classify every task, pick a model, run two
    46	  reviewers, re-attest, keep a ledger, end every reply with a model line —
    47	  measured at **1% compliance** for the last of those. It now says: build
    48	  normally, run the relevant tests, run `coverloop check` before merge/deploy,
    49	  never lower the floor, never self-approve, never bypass red, never send
    50	  secrets or PII. Enforcement lives in code, where it is fail-closed; prose is
    51	  reserved for what no command can enforce.
    52	
    53	This release was itself elevated to **L3**. The path classifier reads it as L2,
    54	but the diff changes the attestation authority itself — baseline validation,
    55	reviewer execution, verdict parsing, and what `SAFE TO MERGE` means — which is
    56	exactly the intent-level risk `--raise-tier --reason` exists for.
    57	
    58	Ten cold review rounds ran against this change; the gate stopped its own merge
    59	every time. 46 findings, all verified against the code, and the pattern in them
    60	decided the shape of the release:
    61	
    62	**Item 2 — "verification is not authorization" — is DEFERRED, not shipped.**
    63	Letting a reviewed-but-uncountersigned commit advance the gate's baseline needs
    64	an obligation to be carried forward instead, and that mechanism produced a
    65	fail-open in **every one of the five rounds**: shed by stacking a commit on top
    66	(round 1), shed under `--human-gate-scope irreversible` (round 2), shed by an L0
    67	successor and by `scope` going retroactively sticky (round 3), lost through
    68	`--base` (round 4), and finally carrying only the human approval while dropping
    69	the reviewer requirement entirely (round 5). Each fix created the next finding.
    70	The baseline therefore stays exactly as released: it advances only under the
    71	full authorization requirements. The fleet's inflated floors remain — that is
    72	over-strictness, which is the safe direction to be wrong in — and item 2 returns
    73	later as an isolated design problem with this history attached.
    74	
    75	What the rounds did close, in what did ship:
    76	
    77	- **The reviewer is named outside the repo, and runs outside it.** Roles and
    78	  commands come from `~/.config/coverloop/reviewers.json` (or
    79	  `$COVERLOOP_REVIEWERS`), never the worktree; `check` refuses a policy that
    80	  resolves inside the repo under review (symlinks included), a
    81	  group/world-writable one, and a `.coverloop/config.json` that defines
    82	  reviewers at all. Token checks alone could not hold — `python3 -m reviewer`
    83	  picks up a planted `reviewer.py`, `sh <scripts/reviewer` hides the path in a
    84	  redirect token — so the command now executes from a scratch directory with
    85	  `PYTHONPATH` dropped and every repo-reachable `PATH` entry filtered out. What
    86	  it needs arrives explicitly: `$COVERLOOP_REPO`, `$COVERLOOP_DIFF`,
    87	  `$COVERLOOP_BASE`. The packet covers the same span the floor was computed
    88	  over, a `git` failure while producing it stops the run rather than handing a
    89	  reviewer an empty diff, and the scratch copy is cleaned up on every path.
    90	- **The verdict is a machine protocol.** Exactly `VERDICT: PASS`, as the
    91	  reviewer's closing word (only a CLI footer may follow), with no other
    92	  `VERDICT:` line anywhere in the transcript — matched case-insensitively so a
    93	  lowercase `verdict: fail` is caught and rejected rather than skipped.
    94	  `PASS WITH RISKS`, `APPROVE`, `LGTM`, a truncated log and silence all fail.
    95	- **`check` holds its own boundary.** It refuses a dirty tree, re-checks the
    96	  tree and HEAD after the tests and reviewers have run (a test that repairs the
    97	  code it tests must not earn `SAFE TO MERGE` for the broken commit being
    98	  shipped), honours attest's exit status so evidence git would ignore stops the
    99	  run, reports exactly one `STOP:` and exit 1 — and in `--json` owns file
   100	  descriptor 1, not just Python's `sys.stdout`, since the test command and the
   101	  reviewers are subprocesses that inherit it.
   102	- **An elevation cannot claim the classifier's exemption.** Under
   103	  `--human-gate-scope irreversible` the exemption reads the classified paths,
   104	  and `--raise-tier` exists precisely because the classifier under-reads the
   105	  change. An elevation's recorded reason also survives the ordinary
   106	  `check` -> `attest --approve` flow instead of being folded back into
   107	  "derived".
   108	- **CI was running 102 of 264 tests.** `unittest.main()` sat above classes
   109	  appended later in `tests/test_properties.py`, and CI invokes that file
   110	  directly — so every regression added in this release was invisible to it. The
   111	  entry point now lives at the end of the file, where what it collects cannot
   112	  silently shrink.
   113	- **A repo could weaken its own test gate.** `test_command` is what
   114	  "tests: pass" *means*, and reviewers only run at L2/L3, so a commit rewriting
   115	  it to `true` was ordinary L1 source by path: no meaningful tests, no review,
   116	  `SAFE TO MERGE`. Classifying the config FILE as L2 was tried first and
   117	  reverted — it sits in the first span of every repo after `init`, so it raised
   118	  the floor fleet-wide on adoption, which is the over-classification this tool
   119	  warns about. `check` now compares the current command against the one the
   120	  last ancestor report actually ran, and raises to L2 when it changed. (Reading
   121	  the baseline's config, or the last report's command, would never fire:
   122	  rewriting the command invalidates that evidence by construction and drops the
   123	  baseline to the root, and a deleted report is indistinguishable from
   124	  first-time setup. It reads the config file's OWN git history instead — one
   125	  commit touching it means adoption, two or more means it changed, and git
   126	  failing to answer fails closed. The promotion is written into the report, not
   127	  just used locally: evaluating L2 while recording L1 left CI's gate reading L1
   128	  and never asking for the transcript.)
   129	- **The review packet shows everything the floor judged.** Excluding all of
   130	  `.coverloop/reports/` hid `.coverloop/reports/evil.sql` — which classifies L3
   131	  like any other path, since only the exact SHA-shaped artifacts are evidence.
   132	  The floor said L3 while both reviewers read a packet without the cause.
   133	- **The test command cannot swap the reviewer out from under the run.** It
   134	  executes first, with the operator's privileges, and can overwrite a user-owned
   135	  reviewer wrapper without touching a single repository file — so the dirty-tree
   136	  check sees nothing. `check` fingerprints each reviewer's resolved executable
   137	  before and after the tests run and stops on a change. This does not make an
   138	  arbitrary test command safe (a gate is not a sandbox); it closes that swap.
   139	- **An approval cannot outlive the claim it was given for.** `attest --approve`
   140	  is allowed at every tier, and raising an existing report's tier kept the
   141	  recorded approval — so an L1 sign-off authorized a later L3 elevation whose
   142	  reason did not exist when it was given. Raising the tier now clears it.
   143	- **Reviewers run BEFORE the repository-controlled tests.** The configured test
   144	  command is arbitrary code with the operator's privileges, and the reviewers
   145	  are what it would most like to replace. Hashing the reviewer executable around
   146	  the test run does not settle it — a fake wrapper restores the original before
   147	  it exits, and only the first command word is ever resolved — so the reviewers
   148	  now run first, in their own attest pass, before any repository-controlled code
   149	  executes. The fingerprints remain as a tripwire for an earlier swap. The
   150	  residual limit is real: an arbitrary test command can do anything to local
   151	  state, and a gate is not a sandbox.
   152	- **The packet is scanned before it leaves, not after.** A reviewer command may
   153	  ship the diff to an external model, but redaction only ran on the way BACK,
   154	  when capturing output — by which point a committed credential had already
   155	  left the machine. The diff is redacted and scanned before any reviewer process
   156	  starts, and a secret that survives redaction stops the run outright.
   157	- **Three guards checked one thing while the runtime did another** — the same
   158	  shape each time. `--base` compared the test command against the config's
   159	  first-ever version rather than the base's, so a branch could RESTORE a
   160	  historical `true` over a base that runs the real suite. The outbound secret
   161	  scan was skipped entirely when the canonical filter could not be imported,
   162	  leaving a fallback that knows neither database URLs nor service-role keys —
   163	  a missing filter now stops the run before any reviewer sees the packet.
   164	  And policy validation checked the literal command token while execution
   165	  resolved it through `PATH`, so `reviewer -> <repo>/scripts/reviewer` on an
   166	  external PATH entry ran repository-controlled code; `check` now resolves what
   167	  will actually run, follows the symlink, and refuses if it lands in the tree.
   168	- **The test suite no longer spends production review budget.** It drives the
   169	  real reviewer CLIs with a stubbed transport — nothing is sent — but
   170	  `log_egress` still wrote `attempt` markers to the operator's production egress
   171	  log, and the daily cap counts exactly those. A full run burned ~30 slots, so
   172	  after a few runs every real review that day was refused; that is why the GLM
   173	  red-team failed closed in four of the five rounds. The suite now runs against
   174	  a temporary log and FAILS if the production one is touched at all.
   175	
   176	Explicitly **not** done in this release, and still open: a signed
   177	`approvers` allowlist, the lineage refactor that would drop the fixed
   178	`codex`/`glm` report keys, item 2 above, and any change to `human_gate_scope`,
   179	`STALE`, or the L3 GLM requirement.
   180	

